 This is *supposed* to be such a list.  It has been such a list in the past.
 It has gone markedly downhill in the last couple of months, however (since
 pay to play was instituted?).  I had saved your message to respond to later,
 but will step up my response to compensate for the rather rude and unhumorous
 response supplied by Mssr Sprong, who, if I am not mistaken is rather a 
 newcomer.
 
 1) Dietary restriction.  There have been several on the list who have adhered
 to, or at leasted tried to adhere to the low calorie diet advocated by
 Walford, among others.  I have read Walford's book on the subject, but am
 unconvinced.  I am also a bodybuilder by avocation, and as such need *more*
 than normal caloric consumption to sustain my gains.  I also consume rather
 more protein than is suggested (about .7 grams per pound of bodyweight).
 However, I *do* restrict my diet insofar as I keep my percentage of calories
 from fat in the 10-15% range.  This means on the average no more than 30-50
 grams of fat per day for my 3000-3500 calorie diet (these are goals... I am
 not religious about it).  I do not eat red meat often, but enjoy the odd
 steak or lamb and indulge myself in this when the mood strikes.  I eat
 quite a bit of fish, chicken and turkey.  There are a number of vegetarians
 and vegans on the list, and this is a topic of frequent conversation and
 a non-trivial amount of controversy.
 
 2) Canadian Drug.  I am not sure what drug you are speaking of here.  Please
 be more specific.  There are a lot of discussions of nootropics here, some
 of which have purported life extending qualities.  There are others with
 more experience here than I have (dVdT?)
 
 3) Free radical stuff.  I assume you are referring to the free radical theory
 of aging advocated most vocally by Pearson and Shaw, as well as by the 
 Life Extension Foundation.  I suppose I qualify as an adherent to this theory.
 I have been on "megadoses" (hate that word) of antioxidant supplements for
 about 10 years now.  I am 33 years old and have skin elasticity of someone
 significantly younger.  I am fairly resistant to disease, indicating an 
 active immune system.  When I first read Pearson&Shaw, I also read a number
 of the primary sources cited in their book.  I found the arguments persuasive
 and the studies by and large well constructed.  I regard my antioxidant
 supplementation as an  experiment that I am conducting on myself.  Thus
 far I am very pleased with the results.  I would be happy to discuss the
 specifics of my regimen if you are interested.
 
 
 I hope this response is helpful.  I am willing to continue a dialogue on
 this or related subject matter, but warn you that I am extremely busy these
 days and so my response time may not be as short as I would like.
 
 I apologize for the flippancy with which your original query was greeted.
 This has not traditionally been the way we deal with such.
 
